% The Ponens policies iosco-supervisory-recordkeeping, as a program of Logical English for LPS.
%
% Written by lpsPlus's ponens_twin.pl (docs/migration/ponens.md) from 11
% Ponens policies, whose files are in sources/ (Ponens: github.com/imandra-ai/
% ponens, MIT licence, Imandra Inc.), with 2 Ponens traces as its scenarios.
%
% Each step of a trace is an action of the session Ponens recorded. Each
% policy is a causal law that records a breach: at the step where the policy
% fails, when the step ends, or at the end of the session when the policy
% looks forward. Where a scenario has a comment above it, the comment is what
% Ponens's own evaluator (`ponens trace check --json`) says of the same
% policies on that trace.
%
% DISCLAIMER. This program was written by a translator from the policies of
% another system. It is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind,
% express or implied, including any warranty that it is accurate, complete or
% fit for a particular purpose. The translation may be wrong: check it against
% its source before relying on it. It is not legal, regulatory or other
% professional advice. Its authors accept no liability for any loss or damage
% arising from its use.

the target language is: lps.

the maximum time is 16.

the events are:
    step *a step* is a *a kind*; known as step_is_a.
    step *a step* shows *a sign*; known as step_shows.
    step *a step* ends; known as step_ends.
    the trace shows *a sign*; known as trace_shows.
    the session ends; known as session_ends.

the fluents are:
    step *a step* was a *a kind*; known as was_a.
    step *a step* has shown *a sign*; known as has_shown.
    step *a step* has ended; known as has_ended.
    the trace has shown *a sign*; known as trace_has_shown.
    step *a step* meets *a condition*; known as meets.
    the policy *a policy* is broken at step *a step*; known as broken_at.

the knowledge base iosco supervisory recordkeeping includes:

% What a trace records, kept to the end of the session.
when step a step is a a kind
then step the step was a the kind.

when step a step shows a sign
then step the step has shown the sign.

when step a step ends
then step the step has ended.

when the trace shows a sign
then the trace has shown the sign.

% ai_content_marked (warning): G(Output ∧ client_facing → marked_ai_generated)
% IOSCO Table 5.1: 'Provide examples of how outputs are marked as AI-generated or synthetic.' Machine-readable labels on AI-generated content. A flag (Amber) where labelling is missing.
when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Output
    and step the step has shown "client facing"
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "marked ai generated"
then the policy ai_content_marked is broken at step the step.

% ai_inventory_recorded (warning): G((Decision ∨ Output) → inventory_recorded)
% IOSCO §3.1(d) / Table 6.1: firms maintain an AI inventory capturing each system's purpose, scope, data and model usage, and dependencies. A flag (Amber) where inventory coverage is incomplete.
step a step meets "is a Decision or is a Output" at a time if
    step the step was a Decision at the time.

step a step meets "is a Decision or is a Output" at a time if
    step the step was a Output at the time.

when step a step ends
    and step the step meets "is a Decision or is a Output"
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "inventory recorded"
then the policy ai_inventory_recorded is broken at step the step.

% ai_outcomes_logged (error): G((Decision ∨ Output) → logged ∧ model_version_recorded)
% IOSCO Table 6.1/6.3: documentation and logs maintained across the lifecycle (design → retirement), including model versioning, to support auditability of AI-generated outcomes.
step a step meets "shows logged and shows model version recorded" at a time if
    step the step has shown "logged" at the time
    and step the step has shown "model version recorded" at the time.

when step a step ends
    and step the step meets "is a Decision or is a Output"
    and it is not the case that step the step meets "shows logged and shows model version recorded"
then the policy ai_outcomes_logged is broken at step the step.

% ai_use_disclosed (error): G(Output ∧ client_facing → ai_disclosed)
% IOSCO Table 5.1: 'How does the firm ensure clients know when AI is generating responses or analysis?' Hidden AI use in investor-facing services is a disclosure failure.
when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Output
    and step the step has shown "client facing"
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "ai disclosed"
then the policy ai_use_disclosed is broken at step the step.

% consent_for_ai_decisions (warning): G(ai_affects_client_rights → P(client_consent))
% IOSCO Table 5.3: where AI systems impact client rights, account access, or privacy, the firm informs clients and seeks explicit consent (opt-in / opt-out recorded).
step a step meets "an earlier step shows client consent" at a time if
    step a second step has ended at the time
    and the second step < the step
    and step the second step has shown "client consent" at the time.

when step a step ends
    and step the step has shown "ai affects client rights"
    and it is not the case that step the step meets "an earlier step shows client consent"
then the policy consent_for_ai_decisions is broken at step the step.

% decision_logic_recorded (error): G(Output → explainability_recorded)
% IOSCO Table 6.4: records that explain how AI systems generate outputs, including decision-making logic and reasoning, tailored for the relevant stakeholders.
when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Output
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "explainability recorded"
then the policy decision_logic_recorded is broken at step the step.

% human_oversight_evidenced (error): G(material_impact → human_reviewed)
% IOSCO Table 6.3: 'How does the firm document and retain evidence of human oversight or intervention in decisions made by AI systems, particularly in investor or market impacting applications?'
when step a step ends
    and step the step has shown "material impact"
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "human reviewed"
then the policy human_oversight_evidenced is broken at step the step.

% incidents_root_caused (error): G(Incident → root_cause_recorded ∧ remediation_recorded)
% IOSCO Table 6.5 / §3.4: negative outcomes should be recorded and root cause analysis performed to determine cause and remediation; detailed records of AI-related incidents are maintained.
step a step meets "shows root cause recorded and shows remediation recorded" at a time if
    step the step has shown "root cause recorded" at the time
    and step the step has shown "remediation recorded" at the time.

when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Incident
    and it is not the case that step the step meets "shows root cause recorded and shows remediation recorded"
then the policy incidents_root_caused is broken at step the step.

% no_ai_washing (error): G(ai_claim → claim_substantiated)
% IOSCO Box 4 / Table 5.4: misleading, inaccurate, or false AI claims ('AI-washing') can be the basis for enforcement. Claims must be evidence-based and substantiated before appearing in disclosures.
when step a step ends
    and step the step has shown "ai claim"
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "claim substantiated"
then the policy no_ai_washing is broken at step the step.

% output_action_traceable (error): G((Release ∨ Deploy) → P(Output ∧ logged))
% IOSCO Table 6.3: 'What processes ensure traceability between AI outputs and final actions taken?' The action must link back to the recorded output that drove it.
step a step meets "is a Release or is a Deploy" at a time if
    step the step was a Release at the time.

step a step meets "is a Release or is a Deploy" at a time if
    step the step was a Deploy at the time.

step a step meets "an earlier step is a Output and shows logged" at a time if
    step a second step has ended at the time
    and the second step < the step
    and step the second step was a Output at the time
    and step the second step has shown "logged" at the time.

when step a step ends
    and step the step meets "is a Release or is a Deploy"
    and it is not the case that step the step meets "an earlier step is a Output and shows logged"
then the policy output_action_traceable is broken at step the step.

% reportable_incident_notified (error): G(Incident ∧ reportable → F(supervisor_notified))
% IOSCO Table 6.6: timely, high-quality reporting; firms notify supervisors of significant AI failures, biases, cybersecurity events, data breaches, or incorrect outputs that meet the reportable threshold.
step a step meets "it or a later step shows supervisor notified" at a time if
    step a second step has ended at the time
    and the second step >= the step
    and step the second step has shown "supervisor notified" at the time.

when the session ends
    and step a step has ended
    and step the step was a Incident
    and step the step has shown "reportable"
    and it is not the case that step the step meets "it or a later step shows supervisor notified"
then the policy reportable_incident_notified is broken at step the step.

scenario governed is:
    % Ponens: every policy of the pack passes on this trace.
    step 1 is a Decision from 2 to 3.
    step 1 shows "inventory recorded" from 2 to 3.
    step 1 shows "logged" from 2 to 3.
    step 1 shows "model version recorded" from 2 to 3.
    step 1 shows "client consent" from 2 to 3.
    step 1 shows "explainability recorded" from 2 to 3.
    step 1 ends from 3 to 4.
    step 2 is a Output from 4 to 5.
    step 2 shows "client facing" from 4 to 5.
    step 2 shows "marked ai generated" from 4 to 5.
    step 2 shows "inventory recorded" from 4 to 5.
    step 2 shows "logged" from 4 to 5.
    step 2 shows "model version recorded" from 4 to 5.
    step 2 shows "ai disclosed" from 4 to 5.
    step 2 shows "explainability recorded" from 4 to 5.
    step 2 ends from 5 to 6.
    step 3 is a Output from 6 to 7.
    step 3 shows "inventory recorded" from 6 to 7.
    step 3 shows "logged" from 6 to 7.
    step 3 shows "model version recorded" from 6 to 7.
    step 3 shows "explainability recorded" from 6 to 7.
    step 3 shows "material impact" from 6 to 7.
    step 3 shows "human reviewed" from 6 to 7.
    step 3 ends from 7 to 8.
    step 4 shows "logged" from 8 to 9.
    step 4 is a Release from 8 to 9.
    step 4 ends from 9 to 10.
    step 5 shows "ai claim" from 10 to 11.
    step 5 shows "claim substantiated" from 10 to 11.
    step 5 ends from 11 to 12.
    step 6 is a Output from 12 to 13.
    step 6 shows "inventory recorded" from 12 to 13.
    step 6 shows "logged" from 12 to 13.
    step 6 shows "model version recorded" from 12 to 13.
    step 6 shows "ai affects client rights" from 12 to 13.
    step 6 shows "explainability recorded" from 12 to 13.
    step 6 ends from 13 to 14.
    the session ends from 14 to 15.

scenario violating is:
    % Ponens (`ponens trace check --json`):
    %     ai_content_marked: failed at step 1
    %     ai_inventory_recorded: failed at step 1
    %     ai_outcomes_logged: failed at step 1
    %     ai_use_disclosed: failed at step 1
    %     decision_logic_recorded: failed at step 1
    %     human_oversight_evidenced: failed at step 3
    %     incidents_root_caused: failed at step 2
    %     no_ai_washing: failed at step 4
    %     output_action_traceable: failed at step 5
    %     reportable_incident_notified: failed at step 2
    step 1 is a Output from 2 to 3.
    step 1 shows "client facing" from 2 to 3.
    step 1 ends from 3 to 4.
    step 2 is a Incident from 4 to 5.
    step 2 shows "reportable" from 4 to 5.
    step 2 ends from 5 to 6.
    step 3 shows "material impact" from 6 to 7.
    step 3 ends from 7 to 8.
    step 4 shows "ai claim" from 8 to 9.
    step 4 ends from 9 to 10.
    step 5 is a Release from 10 to 11.
    step 5 ends from 11 to 12.
    the session ends from 12 to 13.
